# Cloudflare Pages / Netlify `_redirects` file format.
#
# TWO RULES, both terminal 404s, and the SPA fallback is RETIRED — deliberately, at
# T9-W5 §5.1, after four campaigns of a third rule that could never fire.
#
# What was here: `/*  /index.html  200`, described in this header and in README as the
# SPA fallback. It never took effect. Cloudflare Pages serves a `404.html` found in the
# build output for any path that matches no asset, and that answer comes BEFORE the
# `_redirects` splat — so with `public/404.html` shipping alongside it, every bare path
# got the 404 body and the rewrite was dead code (booked at the T7 close record:215,
# measured again on the live edge 2026-08-25: `/`, 200, 3,163 B; `/kenken`,
# `/no-such-path`, `/assets/nope.js`, all 404 with a body byte-identical to 404.html).
#
# Retired rather than cured, because the app has nothing for it to catch: it carries no
# router and mints no path-shaped link. `?game=`, `?view=`, `?board=` and `?s=` are query
# strings on `/`, and the URL grammar touches `searchParams` alone. Only a hand-typed
# path reaches these rules, and the honest answer to a hand-typed path is that it does
# not exist. Restoring the fallback would have meant renaming 404.html out of the slot CF
# reserves and then proving the interaction on the edge — a live behaviour change, bought
# with a deploy, for a case the product never generates.
#
# So the second rule now STATES the 404 instead of inheriting it. The behaviour is
# identical to today's; what changed is that the file no longer claims otherwise, and the
# not-found answer no longer depends on a Cloudflare precedence rule nobody wrote down.
# `scripts/edge-probe.mjs --rows spa-fallback-contract` holds it: a `200` rule under a
# shipped 404.html is a static contradiction and reds, whatever the edge happens to say.
#
# Unknown /assets/* paths are 404s, NEVER the app shell: hashed assets that don't
# exist must not resolve to index.html, because the /assets/* immutable Cache-Control
# in _headers is path-keyed — a mid-deploy fetch through the zone once cached the
# text/html fallback as a stylesheet for a year (the 2026-07-15 poisoning; nosniff
# then refused it and the app ran unstyled at 5 fps). A 404 is cache-safe: edges
# hold it minutes, not a year, and the next retry heals. The rule stays named and first
# even though the second now says the same thing for every other path: it carries the
# poisoning's cure, and a later change to the general rule must not quietly take it away.
/assets/*    /404.html    404

/*    /404.html    404
